Root cause:
The bug was caused by incorrect GPIO pin assignments for the powerdown functionality of the WSA881x audio components in the device tree source (DTS) file. The left speaker was incorrectly configured to use GPIO2 instead of GPIO1, and the right speaker was incorrectly configured to use GPIO3 instead of GPIO2. This misconfiguration led to audio functionality being broken after a fix in the GPIO driver was applied, which corrected a shift bug that had previously allowed the incorrect assignments to work.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
powerdown-gpios = <&wcdgpio 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
powerdown-gpios = <&wcdgpio 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
```
Reason:
The powerdown pins for the WSA881x audio components were incorrectly assigned to GPIO2 for the left speaker and GPIO3 for the right speaker instead of the correct GPIO1 and GPIO2. This misconfiguration caused audio functionality to break after a fix in the GPIO driver was applied, which corrected a shift bug that had previously allowed the incorrect assignments to work.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
powerdown-gpios = <&wcdgpio 1 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
powerdown-gpios = <&wcdgpio 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
```
Reason:
The patch corrects the GPIO pin assignments by setting the left speaker's powerdown pin to GPIO1 and the right speaker's powerdown pin to GPIO2, aligning the configuration with the actual hardware connections and ensuring proper audio functionality.


